**What I tested (deepening track, pre-registered)**
The research lane had concluded that the exit process of wall-to-wall reaccel trades is Markov in the current unrealized R alone. I tried to break that with two path-shape variables that had never been tested: the realized volatility of the path so far and the fraction of bars spent in profit. Both are measurable at bar k, so under a martingale neither should predict the remaining path. The analysis reused only the existing full-path npz cache and ran in 1.9 seconds.

**Result: null on both hypotheses**

| Cell (k=24, near-zero R) | In-sample 2010-20 | Out-of-sample 2021-26H1 |
|---|---|---|
| Path volatility top minus bottom tercile | +0.021R (t +0.37) | −0.262R (t −2.54) |
| Time-in-profit top minus bottom tercile | −0.011R (t −0.19) | −0.131R (t −1.33) |
| Pooled regression beta per 1 sd, volatility | +0.017R (t 1.19) | −0.020R (t −0.57) |

Across 60 cells only one reached nominal significance, against 2.7 expected by chance, and the t-statistics have standard deviation 0.99. The trader lore that a trade which gave back its profit "rots" does not hold. Half of what looks like give-back is just the current R level, since time-in-profit correlates with current R at 0.44 to 0.52 even inside the narrow bin.

**Strongest counter-argument recorded**
The in-sample cell can only detect differences above about 0.11R, so the null says "no 0.1R-class information", not "none". The out-of-sample volatility cell is large at −0.26R and reads as "quiet stalled trades run, turbulent ones die". It is rejected only because the in-sample sign is opposite and the family-wise probability of one such cell among 60 is about 0.49. A forward one-cell check on post-2026-07 data is logged in the backlog.

