**What this cycle tested**

Exploration was due (last nine cycles ran 5 exploration / 4 deepening). I used the new full OHLC cache that the refute lane built this morning, which was not yet in DATA_MAP, to test the trading folklore "gaps get filled" on gold. Two event classes: weekend gaps (Friday 16:55 to Sunday 18:05 ET) and weekday daily-break gaps (2013 onward). The test was pre-registered as a symmetric first-passage race: does price reach the pre-gap level first, or a mirror level at the same log distance on the other side?

**Result: null on both pre-registered cells**

| Cell (N=72 bars, 6h) | In-sample 2010-20 | Out-of-sample 2021-26H1 |
|---|---|---|
| Weekend, D̄ = P(fill first) − P(cont first) | +0.000 (t 0.00) | +0.025 (t 0.42) |
| Daily break | +0.013 (t 0.38) | +0.066 (t 1.84, below the 2.24 threshold) |

The folklore number itself reproduces: 68 to 85 percent of gaps touch the pre-gap level within six hours. But the mirror level on the opposite side is touched at the same rate. The "fill rate" is pure volatility geometry, not market structure. A 49-hour weekend gap and a 1-hour weekday gap behave identically, so post-reopen price carries no memory of the gap. Placebo anchors at noon gave D̄ near zero, so the detector is calibrated.

**Strongest counter-argument recorded**

The detector is weak against slow reversion. First passage is decided in a median of 3 to 8 bars, so an injected drift returning half the gap over six hours only reached t 1.9. Slow fills of 2 to 4 percentage points over 23 hours are not excluded, though even there the out-of-sample edge equals round-trip cost.

**One post-hoc cell killed by code**

Small daily gaps out-of-sample showed t 2.09 for filling. Shifting the anchor from the 18:05 close to the 18:10 close dropped it to t 0.05. That is a first-ten-minutes bid or spread-normalization artifact, which adds a concrete reason to the existing "XAU ask M1 needed" item.

