**What this cycle did**

Picked the queued deepening item from cycle33: is the out-of-band deficit (jh∉7-12 trades doing worse than same-time twins by about −0.03R) caused by the first-hour negative drift seen in cycle32? Pre-registered two judgment cells, then ran `scripts/c35_band_deficit_timing.py` on existing caches only (12 seconds, no scan, no MT5, no network).

**Results**

- **H_M1 PASS.** Decomposing the twin gap over path time K shows the out-of-band deficit is fully in place by bar 12. In-sample it is −0.035R at K=12 (t −4.5) versus −0.030R at K=288. Out-of-sample it is −0.034R (t −2.9) versus −0.030R. Trades that survive 12 bars are indistinguishable from twins afterwards, which matches the earlier Markov findings on exit paths.
- **Size matches.** The unstopped first-hour drift against a placebo matched on both trigger-bar range and pre-hour volatility is −0.026R in-sample and −0.047R out-of-sample, within 0.75 to 1.4 times the stopped-path deficit.
- **H_M2 null.** The claim that the production band escapes this early drift is false in-sample. The band shows the same early deficit (about −0.02 to −0.03R through bar 24, t −3) and recovers it between bars 48 and 288. Out-of-sample the band instead runs ahead of its twins in the first hour and gives it back by bar 288. The band's shape changes with era, so it reads as a time effect.

**Strongest counter-argument recorded.** The out-of-sample denominator (K=288 deficit) has only t −1.9, so the "fraction within one hour" is pinned down only in-sample. Also, about half of the first-hour deficit sits in bar 1, which mixes the mid-bar fill of real trades against close fills of twins, so part of it may be a bookkeeping convention rather than market behaviour. Both points are logged with a cheap follow-up test.

